{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-17T13:31:02Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-escape-literals-self-inflict","ref_id":"docs-model-nul-escape","severity":"P3","finding":"writing about a control-character escape reliably inserts the control character itself into the prose","decision":"Three times in twenty minutes on 2026-08-17 a raw NUL byte landed in a file while documenting the fix that removes raw NUL bytes: the test comment, the intake Summary, and an earlier command. Discipline is not the control here; the byte count is. A cheap guard would be a repo-wide check that no tracked text file contains a NUL, which would also have caught the original defect years earlier","source":"observed while shipping docs-model-nul-escape, 2026-08-17"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-17T15:42:17Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-no-nul-guard","ref_id":"docs-model-nul-escape","severity":"P2","finding":"nothing checks tracked text files for NUL bytes, so a control character can silently turn a source file binary to POSIX grep","decision":"CHANGE-0147 found two NUL bytes in a shared library that had silently disarmed the negative taxonomy guard in test-aai-delta-stage2.sh line 210 (proven with a planted canary: the guard logged PASS while finding nothing). git grep and ripgrep still match, but the repo's own suites use POSIX grep, so the failure lands exactly where it is least visible. A repo-wide no-NUL check would have caught the original and all three relapses while writing the fix","source":"code review of PR 262, docs/ai/reviews/review-docs-model-nul-escape-20260817T153931Z.md"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-17T22:18:30Z","actor":"remediation","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-scope-cost-no-live-view","ref_id":"ride-cost-readout","severity":"P3","finding":"scope_cost rows only exist after metrics-flush and the report only regenerates at close, so the section cannot serve the intake's headline motivation (deciding to stop a ride while it is still running)","decision":"agent_runs lands in METRICS.jsonl only at flush and the report is regenerated best-effort at close-work-item.mjs; a live in-progress view would need a different data source (e.g. STATE.yaml's not-yet-flushed agent_runs) and is a separate, unshipped capability. Nothing false ships today, but the limit was unstated; the product doc's Limits section now names it explicitly","source":"docs/ai/reviews/review-dual-verdict-ride-cost-readout-20260817T220719Z.md NB-8"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-18T07:05:30.000Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"process_finding","ref_id":"ride-cost-readout","finding":"the TEST-011 branch-diff allowlist tax is structurally invisible to the lightweight lane","detail":"CHANGE-0148 is ceremony L1, so validation covers the declared scope only and never runs tests/skills/test-aai-spec-lint.sh, where the pin lives. The failure therefore surfaced 20 minutes into CI rather than in validation. The four earlier payments (CHANGE-0143, 0145, 0146, 0147) were all heavy-lane rides whose mandatory full sweep caught it locally. Every future L1 ride touching any .aai/ path will hit this the same way.","relates_to":"fu-test011-branch-diff-allowlist-tax"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-18T07:13:38Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-validation-ignores-suite-selector","ref_id":"ride-cost-readout","severity":"P2","finding":"validation picks its suites from the declared review scope while CI picks them from select-suites, so a CORE suite can be skipped in validation and fail in CI","decision":"On CHANGE-0148 the selector marks aai-spec-lint CORE for any change, but the L1 validation dispatch told the role that the owning suite plus siblings were the relevant surface, so spec-lint never ran and TEST-011 failed 20 minutes into CI instead. The orchestrator wrote that instruction, so the fix is a rule not a reminder: a validation dispatch should run what select-suites returns for the changed files, never a hand-picked list","source":"PR 263 CI failure 2026-08-18, select-suites output CORE aai-spec-lint"}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-18T07:13:38.000Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"process_finding","ref_id":"ride-cost-readout","finding":"correction to the earlier process_finding on this ref","detail":"The earlier entry blamed the lightweight lane for hiding the TEST-011 allowlist tax. That was wrong: select-suites marks aai-spec-lint CORE for every change, so CI routing was correct. The real cause is that the validation dispatch hand-picked the suites to run, and the orchestrator wrote that instruction. Superseded by fu-validation-ignores-suite-selector."}
{"v":1,"ts":"2026-08-18T07:14:52Z","actor":"orchestrator","type":"follow_up","id":"fu-orchestrator-does-not-watch-ci","ref_id":"ride-cost-readout","severity":"P2","finding":"after pushing a PR the orchestrator stops instead of watching CI, so a failure sits undetected until the owner asks","decision":"On PR 263 the full framework failed around 04:52 and the orchestrator learned of it at 07:02 when the owner asked — two hours and ten minutes of dead time. Every CI check today was owner-prompted, never self-initiated. Waiting is not watching. The fix is mechanical: after a push, poll the checks to settlement in the background rather than idling, the same discipline already demanded of subagents waiting on a long run","source":"PR 263, push 04:25, failure 04:52, detection 07:02"}
